expanded_source_wording_tex:
\label{prop:d1-laws}
Let \(q:X\to A\), \(r:X\to B\), and \(s:X\to C\) be quotient maps, represented
by their fibers.  Then:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \(q\vee r\preceq q\) and \(q\vee r\preceq r\);
  \item if \(t\preceq q\) and \(t\preceq r\), then \(t\preceq q\vee r\);
  \item \(q\vee q\) is fiber-equivalent to \(q\);
  \item \(q\vee r\) is fiber-equivalent to \(r\vee q\); and
  \item \((q\vee r)\vee s\) is fiber-equivalent to \(q\vee(r\vee s)\); and
  \item replacing \(q\) or \(r\) by a fiber-equivalent quotient
  presentation leaves the join fiber-equivalent to the original join.
\end{enumerate}

